# rules: kotlin-world-readable-storage
# languages: kotlin
# fixture: kotlin/CWE-276.kt
# from: CWE-276 (Incorrect Default Permissions), CWE-732
#
# An app's files directory is private to that app, and these two modes give
# that away. A file opened MODE_WORLD_READABLE can be read by any app on the
# phone with no permission and no prompt; MODE_WORLD_WRITEABLE lets one rewrite
# it, which turns a cache into a way to feed the app whatever it likes.
#
# They were deprecated in API 17 and throw a SecurityException from API 24, so
# a program that still names them either predates that or never runs on a
# modern phone - and either way the intent to share a private file is the
# finding, whatever the platform now does with it.
#
# The mode is read as text rather than as a number, because it is written as a
# constant almost everywhere and the numeric spellings (1 and 2) are also
# ordinary array indexes and lengths. Reading it as a number would report every
# `openFileOutput(name, 1)` in the corpus and find nothing this does not.

["$C.openFileOutput($N, $M)", "$C.getSharedPreferences($N, $M)",
 "$C.getDir($N, $M)", "$C.openOrCreateDatabase($N, $M, $$$_)"] as $opens
| "\\bMODE_WORLD_(?:READABLE|WRITEABLE|WRITABLE)\\b" as $shared
| scan_ast("*.kt"; $opens) as $all
| ($all | of($opens) | where_capture("M"; $shared) | focus("M"))
| finding("kotlin-world-readable-storage";
    "this opens $N with $M, so every other app on the phone can get at it")
| report
